  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 130 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... We will start the day with plenty of stratus and \nfog. Then, scattered PM thunderstorms today will be limited to the \nSE Coastal Plain. \n\nA weak cold front has moved south through the region and will become \nstationary near the coast this afternoon. Plenty of low level \nmoisture remained across central NC as the wind has become light \neasterly. The stratus will become rather extensive in the next few \nhours and is forecast to linger well into the mid to late morning. \nOtherwise, much drier air in the mid-levels associated with high \npressure will suppress the typical afternoon thunderstorm chance \nover the Piedmont and much of NC. However, the weak boundary near \nthe coast will combine with the sea breeze to produce a few \nthunderstorms into far SE NC. It will be a bit cooler many areas \ntoday with the morning clouds and light E flow. Highs generally in \nthe mid to upper 80s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... High heat and humidity return quickly this week, \nbeginning Monday and peaking Wednesday through Friday.  \n\nStrong ridging is expected to build aloft from the hot and dry \nsouthern Plains east to the Atlantic coast into Wednesday, then \nbecome nearly stationary over the TN Valley and southern Mid-\nAtlantic mid to late week. \n\nThis essentially means an early September heat wave with highs \nreturning to the 90-95 range Monday and well into the 90s most areas \nTue-Friday. The heat indices will likely reach 105 by Tuesday, \npeaking between 105 and 110 Wednesday into Friday. Heat advisories \nwill be likely.\n\nOther than an isolated storm or two during peak heating, expect very \nlow POP for any storms this week. QPF will be less than 0.10 on \naverage for the next 5 days.",
